 ## Background
-**Origin:**
-The method was introduced in the paper "Neural Scene Graphs for Dynamic Scenes" [S1].
-
-**History and Development:**
-The work addresses limitations in existing neural rendering methods. SRNs optimize to a single static representation, failing for dynamic scenes. NeRF suffers from ghosting due to camera pose changes. This method was developed to reconstruct each object individually to improve temporal consistency and enable scene manipulation [S1].
-
-**Where it is used:**
-Evaluated on dynamic tracking sequences from the KITTI dataset [S1].
+The method was introduced in "Neural Scene Graphs for Dynamic Scenes" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of existing neural rendering techniques for dynamic environments, specifically the inability of SRNs to handle temporal changes and the ghosting artifacts of NeRF when camera poses vary. The work demonstrates the flexibility of the neural scene graph representation by manipulating edges and nodes to preserve illumination cues and compose novel scenes.
 
